99635 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499633 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186499436 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499633 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186499437 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499633 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186499438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499633 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186499439 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499633 #edb/ref 1031 "Approximating Square Roots and Cube Roots" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499633 #edb/ref 1033 492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499633 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "76fea41a-98af-5c68-b72e-6881d877a1e9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499635 #edb/ref 1064 "When we **estimate** a root, we choose one nearby decimal value and check that it is reasonable. Bounds tell us the interval; the estimate tells us which value in that interval is closest.\n\nLet's estimate ![](images/39/398d865ecee9dba51e8ba4918a187f483b9291ff6ae0d2032be0d95d40190ff7.svg) rounded to ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg) decimal place. First, we find nearby perfect cubes:\n![](images/bc/bca4e36ccfb33070d91ecf1f82eab6ce7d8e06f71d05000633a58ac03ce19107.svg)\nso\n![](images/cd/cdf283b9d6e8644576f4e97abe9203e4115c00fd43314a2323706d1c643e9c0b.svg)\n\n![](images/36/3636292deb3377b1dfe99da068f300f7dba4ce7fa6acd22164150451dc396ee1.png)\n\nBecause ![](images/bc/bc98a2fdf686f851fd343e6cca8ebf25186c6d4fe401df573939f8e5e1d5d044.svg) is closer to ![](images/c6/c64ba31f35ed2d1f23f3827854effc6461b82c7cfa7f27694bf545acb565c90a.svg) than to ![](images/f9/f9e10c6abbe30486481349d2f58357e01d37bfd670ed37a29313e8556b57fe2c.svg) we expect ![](images/39/398d865ecee9dba51e8ba4918a187f483b9291ff6ae0d2032be0d95d40190ff7.svg) to be closer to ![](images/40/402ff5d0c0c3aaa47f18c89f6d96c95d47840eac13822d596a51bdb0c5f9a4ab.svg) than to ![](images/9f/9fc073d1e6ebde82eb949a875e40be00b9196e9b14473ee13fa47a26a98d8450.svg) Now, we test nearby tenths:\n![](images/47/4703877c1fc434e00d2fc781d77473c9ce8f775257b953203baa76d914c2458b.svg)\n\nSo, ![](images/5d/5dfbe3c8f263be79d4c240a343aeef3ed741dc5d9a72b0b8c21b38cd9548a66c.svg) To decide which tenth is the better estimate, compare distances from ![](images/3b/3b3b7808b8023e6edb41eb64eb15e0198ce6843ff72ec8c44e79786e8932dc84.svg)\n![](images/60/60b754ce1636a8fee75de533c7961f498ac37fd7f6b471971e420985069f3c22.svg)\n\nSince ![](images/bc/bc98a2fdf686f851fd343e6cca8ebf25186c6d4fe401df573939f8e5e1d5d044.svg) is closer to ![](images/92/9252231dcb743cf6f0c6df56fdcc7e3f30a9c4d371ad30332ac7a5f93b38b3e4.svg) the root is closer to ![](images/10/106a07431c2edab74f94a851a8f9d6d4c67fb6e29b7c2090e316e5c2032195e3.svg) Therefore,\n![](images/5d/5d0fdcc02424b0794c647227784e86791079bbdbc184868c2bbbdec3ddcf86cb.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499635 #edb/ref 1065 23017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499635 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "199fa1b4-f88c-51c4-a84f-275792b90dee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499635 #edb/ref 1067 "Estimating Roots" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499636 #edb/ref 1064 "Some roots are perfect. For example, ![](images/38/389d3c3681c9e50a78b79c73049365330340f56d1dd87adfa83ed9cc3e3eefed.svg) and ![](images/47/4788ae8534df07b8199121e142f69086fd3aec0f87f201fcf4997831c0b60002.svg) Other roots are not whole numbers, but we can still describe where they belong.\n\nAn **approximation** is a nearby value that helps us understand the size of a number. To approximate roots, we compare the number under the radical to nearby perfect powers.\n\nFor a square root, we compare to perfect squares. Since ![](images/96/96d11100098752a9a927d8b25751f689094ffee73faec61b29b79e0afa375a42.svg) we know\n![](images/49/49fbd1142d93edad07ad98c73bc1dafb04d1e8b5a4820274f51d8cee4e3dfbd3.svg)\n\nFor a cube root, we compare to perfect cubes. Since ![](images/2d/2de677effff1f2ce5ed73724bfdd3bead3f0baab3bac33ed272af03361ec3573.svg) we know\n![](images/7e/7ec574473402909905dc4960cca1ab56bb5790c5fcd80f4ad78cd3b9954dc925.svg)\n\nSo, the main idea is to find familiar benchmark roots first. Once we know the root’s whole-number interval, we can order roots, refine to decimal intervals, and choose reasonable estimates." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499636 #edb/ref 1065 23022 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499636 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4dbd01ab-76e0-5e52-b2bb-2efdd00a2058" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499636 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499637 #edb/ref 1064 "When we order irrational roots, we can often compare their whole-number intervals first. A root between ![](images/0c/0cf2eee0cdec31b7acdc244a255aaee7d6d520cfeee67b64ac9cf215f5377477.svg) and ![](images/37/37e17c4a2302e6352fb7186621c9c43bb5e99d43a8a0bc700c0181d36c6cc915.svg) is greater than any root between ![](images/40/402ff5d0c0c3aaa47f18c89f6d96c95d47840eac13822d596a51bdb0c5f9a4ab.svg) and ![](images/85/85ab4d14e4ae710116ac84de4b03b3cf35723ab969b7abfba97889c497974147.svg)\n\nSuppose we want to identify the greatest number in this list:\n![](images/3b/3b5235ec98b311f2a082eeb8a35ba2e606f452a5467707a2125ce3f27fd26484.svg)\n\nWe first bracket each root.\n\n- ![](images/04/04217800f61f8a2b9dfc2b4efb67a08c1d9f5ef7e365a5ddae4d163146bc0158.svg) lies between ![](images/0c/0cf2eee0cdec31b7acdc244a255aaee7d6d520cfeee67b64ac9cf215f5377477.svg) and ![](images/b1/b1288d1c08b90d72fcd319980da7943ea4941b9f259ece4086d8b24b1bc0cfb2.svg) since ![](images/a3/a34f852d765024d8ae773b8debb02adc77fc371c656b049104a9330dc604f5a4.svg)\n\n- ![](images/5a/5aa880157a445c826aa5e51bfbdf4f3c5ab83afdadf97fc6f533aa6103d28640.svg) lies between ![](images/0c/0cf2eee0cdec31b7acdc244a255aaee7d6d520cfeee67b64ac9cf215f5377477.svg) and ![](images/b1/b1288d1c08b90d72fcd319980da7943ea4941b9f259ece4086d8b24b1bc0cfb2.svg) since ![](images/64/6442e0ee53f3ae690132986dfe60822810ae2f586296bccecba0ab6a07c871db.svg)\n\n- ![](images/39/398d865ecee9dba51e8ba4918a187f483b9291ff6ae0d2032be0d95d40190ff7.svg) lies between ![](images/6c/6cb44f25e489afb06ec62d2e95c4d235ece77eb36e2327b856264d2fb8b22c21.svg) and ![](images/39/39c9d5c6b02f72e65180a1a782af0404d25977c3627c5778e1bcb676286506c4.svg) since ![](images/5b/5bf4ba43a56be76e42af12ac6d00913b93191cb348019c518a60bb6490d412ac.svg)\n\n- ![](images/55/556af15807fb8546143a7adb4457089c3f367764397c6cf94654f30200177aba.svg) lies between ![](images/40/402ff5d0c0c3aaa47f18c89f6d96c95d47840eac13822d596a51bdb0c5f9a4ab.svg) and ![](images/e9/e94ce8a42ab6b97ee9c979dcc0a002b557e1c6339e5950f601a66a9af91f67e5.svg) since ![](images/65/65aec0b2f64e5e7991650650102102099e40d0fa3f33b9951e7f42d02bbba8d2.svg)\n\n- ![](images/ba/ba20e4e917e21ab13b9aab507e09632255fccd6346f08f31333fffd468f3eea1.svg) lies between ![](images/40/402ff5d0c0c3aaa47f18c89f6d96c95d47840eac13822d596a51bdb0c5f9a4ab.svg) and ![](images/e9/e94ce8a42ab6b97ee9c979dcc0a002b557e1c6339e5950f601a66a9af91f67e5.svg) since ![](images/f4/f4638dd2c251dd734b480e1bcaedeb9e1bee07e2f03ff4c85de10287cae9957b.svg)\n\nThe greatest value must be one of the roots between ![](images/0c/0cf2eee0cdec31b7acdc244a255aaee7d6d520cfeee67b64ac9cf215f5377477.svg) and ![](images/b1/b1288d1c08b90d72fcd319980da7943ea4941b9f259ece4086d8b24b1bc0cfb2.svg) so it is either ![](images/04/04217800f61f8a2b9dfc2b4efb67a08c1d9f5ef7e365a5ddae4d163146bc0158.svg) or ![](images/8b/8bdb2119f2b5ebc9f21dcc182d6b68e8e964156168a62cacb6048ca84b6e4dbf.svg) Since ![](images/eb/eb86e1418a307c988f3b246894cfdf9d0e9c8bbceda1641fb831d30fe9b6fd3b.svg) we know ![](images/45/45ee1b9d94d27812c364bf34c46e918d9c2df4b254adbf86932c2d350cde0822.svg)\n\nTherefore, the greatest number is ![](images/8b/8bdb2119f2b5ebc9f21dcc182d6b68e8e964156168a62cacb6048ca84b6e4dbf.svg)\n\n**Watch out!** We can compare radicands directly only when the roots are the same kind. For example, comparing ![](images/5a/5aa880157a445c826aa5e51bfbdf4f3c5ab83afdadf97fc6f533aa6103d28640.svg) and ![](images/04/04217800f61f8a2b9dfc2b4efb67a08c1d9f5ef7e365a5ddae4d163146bc0158.svg) is safe because both are square roots." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499637 #edb/ref 1065 23019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499637 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "52ad8c4f-2f3c-5dcf-8ac3-f9cfff01832e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499637 #edb/ref 1067 "Ordering Irrational Roots" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499638 #edb/ref 1064 "**Decimal bounds** are nearby decimal values that trap a root in a smaller interval, such as between two consecutive tenths.\n\nLet's find the two consecutive tenths between which ![](images/75/75690cbc152b11d764581bab44be87acabbbf69ccaca10786de7fd41462db7c1.svg) lies. First, we find the whole-number bounds:\n![](images/67/670a358c4cfbfff0330b32c236f83d3cf1b52475f6c624873a54bc335aa11b26.svg)\nso\n![](images/0f/0f2da556fe760c541b67a1f219a28784b92ab589f10c67d8bdefe8d4a27548e3.svg)\n\nNow, we test tenths between ![](images/6c/6cb44f25e489afb06ec62d2e95c4d235ece77eb36e2327b856264d2fb8b22c21.svg) and ![](images/40/402ff5d0c0c3aaa47f18c89f6d96c95d47840eac13822d596a51bdb0c5f9a4ab.svg) by squaring them. Since ![](images/75/75690cbc152b11d764581bab44be87acabbbf69ccaca10786de7fd41462db7c1.svg) is close to ![](images/3f/3f1007009d0a0d180a52510e7c9ce724113d2deb10051c26452457286845c91c.svg) we can test ![](images/52/521ed5d9c72e7dc13edf7e79df2272294cd869bd5de324f0f7828e2c888c326c.svg) and ![](images/22/227c2d0283023dacad68c1e78194ec0be80b93d52bffea8bd61b5bafc99102df.svg)\n![](images/66/662e926243f31e64daaa74edc74dd4ef9d78e4d24260995fa38db61be6d5cecb.svg)\n\nSince ![](images/e2/e2574f61b59164e433af2aeeb4c0dd160bf84436387873b2af855fa2770b5753.svg) falls between these square values, we have\n![](images/26/26bc3e1ccf6f626db492303aa9d1493c863cf8a10a2e1e46151f1c89ce86c0bd.svg)\n\nTaking square roots gives\n![](images/d1/d1aa32699a90c28680f86d47dca927f92f769ed1e879af7972a3a97030df30db.svg)\n\n![](images/47/4729d848e2e66944d7e2da9f8fc7655a93a59ac5ee20e9454f95b583b39cd8ee.png)\n\nTherefore, ![](images/75/75690cbc152b11d764581bab44be87acabbbf69ccaca10786de7fd41462db7c1.svg) lies between the consecutive tenths ![](images/52/521ed5d9c72e7dc13edf7e79df2272294cd869bd5de324f0f7828e2c888c326c.svg) and ![](images/22/227c2d0283023dacad68c1e78194ec0be80b93d52bffea8bd61b5bafc99102df.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499638 #edb/ref 1065 23018 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499638 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "63caa2e6-2c19-5afe-93e1-8e484d252e52" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499638 #edb/ref 1067 "Finding Decimal Bounds for Roots" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499640 #edb/ref 1064 "**Bounds** are values that trap a number between a lower value and an upper value. For roots, **whole-number bounds** are consecutive whole numbers that the root lies between.\n\nTo find whole-number bounds, we compare the number under the radical to nearby perfect squares or perfect cubes.\n\nLet's locate ![](images/db/dbd2aff681e9d97891999f08502f4f6e7f41f487b237d6c90e18afa1f77755ef.svg) The nearby perfect squares are ![](images/24/24341571e162e3b4e2ca76590f0ed2e6d8b180365fba30c3fb9ea6041d38179a.svg) and ![](images/3c/3cba1142c4b937d3598aaaed096f8b95178d8c45baf39fb7f9f02f21a21480df.svg) because\n![](images/09/09aad9428a24389e5a19034319e2737d08cf5167bf424e1b78cf0f0e69e49102.svg)\n\nSince ![](images/11/119ef80e819318a00b93e3c34aaca245545c965604be3489dd3529df1232a453.svg) is between these perfect squares, we have\n![](images/8f/8fbdaf9d9bd26df7fe6d1c1ad4403f07ba47a94f1a7f8b0d4e728b6865a7cb23.svg)\n\nTaking the square root of each part gives\n![](images/c9/c9753a4537cde93f9b0a994a0c50bdc6528dba6926bdd021d0cc2b54732277ba.svg)\n\n![](images/44/448579015986d36689743ffeadb6a8b05c96b917778d63a0de7e4a2dd0487370.png)\n\nSo, ![](images/e8/e8219f12c397796f3e761fb39940934f3f6ff51cb7dd3656018be76bcf7db1ba.svg) lies between the consecutive whole numbers ![](images/0c/0cf2eee0cdec31b7acdc244a255aaee7d6d520cfeee67b64ac9cf215f5377477.svg) and ![](images/fe/fe34f715005da1a8b682c9650a76785673fdf25c3b7c7546bb94824470c54caf.svg) The same idea works for cube roots, except we compare to nearby perfect cubes instead." #edb/ref 17592