045417] [17592186558456 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186558202 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558456 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ce2d1112-d79c-5535-b77b-b1cd4861b280" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558463 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186558087 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558463 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558463 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558463 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186558087 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558463 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "da3c162f-11dc-5f98-8e5a-a200564ea1fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558513 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186558463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558513 #edb/ref 1069 "q-556464" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558513 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558513 #edb/ref 1071 "Estimate the value of $\\sqrt{45},$ rounded to $1$ decimal place.\n\n$\\sqrt{45}≈$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558513 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4ce3eb2d-c05e-546e-b2ae-aed79d4b89b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558513 #edb/ref 1074 "To estimate $\\sqrt{45},$ we first find the two perfect squares closest to $45.$\n\nThe closest perfect squares are $36={6}^{2}$ and $49={7}^{2}.$\n\nSince $36<45<49,$ we know that the square root of $45$ lies between $6$ and $7.$\n\n![](images/f9/f9d2536ecb1a4d163997e6415b21ff95094074c7fb521a535fc41d33bb0f0173.png)\n\nSince $45$ lies between $36$ and $49,$ we expect $\\sqrt{45}$ to lie between $6$ and $7.$\n\nLet's test decimals near the middle by squaring them:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{6.7}^{2} & =44.89 \\\\ {6.8}^{2} & =46.24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that $45$ lies between $44.89$ and $46.24,$ so $6.7<\\sqrt{45}<6.8.$\n\nNext, we determine which value is closer to $45:$\n\n- The distance from $45$ to ${6.7}^{2}$ is $45-44.89=0.11.$\n\n- The distance from $45$ to ${6.8}^{2}$ is $46.24-45=1.24.$\n\nSince $45$ is much closer to $44.89,$ $\\sqrt{45}$ is closer to $6.7.$\n\nTherefore, rounded to $1$ decimal place, $\\sqrt{45}≈6.7.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558519 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186558395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558519 #edb/ref 1069 "q-556454" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558519 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558519 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following lists $\\sqrt{7},$ $\\sqrt{3},$ and $\\sqrt{23}$ in order from least to greatest?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558519 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "60620631-f27c-5865-a3ad-1d8af90c2532" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558519 #edb/ref 1074 "To order the irrational numbers $\\sqrt{3},$ $\\sqrt{7},$ and $\\sqrt{23}$ from least to greatest, we can find the consecutive whole numbers between which each square root lies.\n\n- For $\\sqrt{3},$ the closest perfect squares are $1$ and $4.$ Since $1<3<4,$ we have $\\sqrt{1}<\\sqrt{3}<\\sqrt{4},$ which means $1<\\sqrt{3}<2.$\n\n- For $\\sqrt{7},$ the closest perfect squares are $4$ and $9.$ Since $4<7<9,$ we have $\\sqrt{4}<\\sqrt{7}<\\sqrt{9},$ which means $2<\\sqrt{7}<3.$\n\n- For $\\sqrt{23},$ the closest perfect squares are $16$ and $25.$ Since $16<23<25,$ we have $\\sqrt{16}<\\sqrt{23}<\\sqrt{25},$ which means $4<\\sqrt{23}<5.$\n\nComparing these bounds, we can see that the intervals do not overlap and fall into increasing ranges. This gives the order\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3}<\\sqrt{7}<\\sqrt{23}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the list in order from least to greatest is $\\sqrt{3},$ $\\sqrt{7},$ $\\sqrt{23}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558560 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186558456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558560 #edb/ref 1069 "q-556455" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558560 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558560 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following lists $\\sqrt{29},$ $\\sqrt{10},$ and $\\sqrt{6}$ in order from least to greatest?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558560 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c3b24341-9377-52eb-a66f-7900d0e4a928" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558560 #edb/ref 1074 "To order the irrational numbers $\\sqrt{6},$ $\\sqrt{10},$ and $\\sqrt{29}$ from least to greatest, we can find the consecutive whole numbers between which each square root lies.\n\n- For $\\sqrt{6},$ the closest perfect squares are $4$ and $9.$ Since $4<6<9,$ we have $\\sqrt{4}<\\sqrt{6}<\\sqrt{9},$ which means $2<\\sqrt{6}<3.$\n\n- For $\\sqrt{10},$ the closest perfect squares are $9$ and $16.$ Since $9<10<16,$ we have $\\sqrt{9}<\\sqrt{10}<\\sqrt{16},$ which means $3<\\sqrt{10}<4.$\n\n- For $\\sqrt{29},$ the closest perfect squares are $25$ and $36.$ Since $25<29<36,$ we have $\\sqrt{25}<\\sqrt{29}<\\sqrt{36},$ which means $5<\\sqrt{29}<6.$\n\nComparing these bounds, we can see that the intervals do not overlap and fall into increasing ranges. This gives the order\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{6}<\\sqrt{10}<\\sqrt{29}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the list in order from least to greatest is $\\sqrt{6},$ $\\sqrt{10},$ $\\sqrt{29}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558569 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186558447 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558569 #edb/ref 1069 "q-556448" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558569 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558569 #edb/ref 1071 "Between which two consecutive tenths does $\\sqrt[3]{80}$ lie?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558569 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d76dfed4-6285-5a3d-a934-894e3d9dca0a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558569 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the two consecutive tenths between which $\\sqrt[3]{80}$ lies, we need to find two numbers with one decimal place whose cubes bracket $80$.\n\nFirst, let's establish between which two consecutive integers $\\sqrt[3]{80}$ lies.\nWe know that ${4}^{3}=64$ and ${5}^{3}=125$.\nSince $64<80<125$, we know that $4<\\sqrt[3]{80}<5$.\n\nNext, we can test decimals between $4$ and $5$ to narrow down the interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4.2}^{3} & =74.088 \\\\ {4.3}^{3} & =79.507 \\\\ {4.4}^{3} & =85.184\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $79.507<80<85.184$, we can write:\n\n$$\n{4.3}^{3}<80<{4.4}^{3}\n$$\n\nTaking the cube root of each part of the inequality gives:\n\n$$\n4.3<\\sqrt[3]{80}<4.4\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\sqrt[3]{80}$ lies between $4.3$ and $4.4$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558576 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186558375 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558576 #edb/ref 1069 "q-556460" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558576 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558576 #edb/ref 1071 "Between which two consecutive whole numbers does $\\sqrt{55}$ lie?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558576 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f8c35ff1-30ac-5b47-a7ed-d4cc77e1a1ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558576 #edb/ref 1074 "To find which two consecutive whole numbers $\\sqrt{55}$ lies between, we first identify the perfect squares that are just below and just above $55.$\n\nThe perfect squares closest to $55$ are $49$ and $64.$ We have\n\n$$\n49<55<64.\n$$\n\nTaking the square root of each number, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{49} & <\\sqrt{55}<\\sqrt{64} \\\\ 7 & <\\sqrt{55}<8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/71/71bc6c197bd0fb3444a7105d8da77ead56cde1da0bb76139668778bfb4b3cd22.png)\n\nTherefore, $\\sqrt{55}$ lies between $7$ and $8.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558621 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186558655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558621 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "52f3f3ea-f558-5204-97f0-7022de7957db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558621 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186558519 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558650 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186558680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558650 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "80ae3feb-47a4-5788-97ef-06219325a14d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558650 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186558576 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558655 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186558650 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558655 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "897227c1-3d61-59d5-9e70-5da99c446a06" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558655 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186558560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558679 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186558621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558679 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b501d759-0ff7-564e-814a-f284686be63a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558679 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186558569 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558680 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b6977ec2-5812-5040-851f-8cbab1392f10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558680 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186558513 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
